---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.2 — for Loops &amp; The EncounterSim Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.8 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> When you know the exact range of
    rooms, turns, or indices to visit, a <code>for</code> loop packs initialization, termination
    condition, and step update into a single header. Today you will build
    <code>EncounterSim.java</code> — an AP FRQ-style dungeon simulator that tests accumulators,
    streak tracking, and multi-condition room hazards.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/EncounterSim.java" download>EncounterSim.java</a> — bare-minimum
            compiling stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/EncounterSimCheck.java" download>EncounterSimCheck.java</a> —
            automated AP-style test suite (16 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac EncounterSim.java EncounterSimCheck.java
java EncounterSimCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>totalScaledXP(int startFloor, int endFloor, int bossMultiplier)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Compute the total XP earned by clearing every floor from <code>startFloor</code> through
        <code>endFloor</code>, <strong>inclusive</strong> (if <code>startFloor &gt; endFloor</code>,
        return <code>0</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>A standard floor <code>f</code> awards <code>f * 100</code> XP.</li>
        <li>Every 5th floor (when <code>f % 5 == 0</code>) is a Boss Floor and awards
            <code>f * 100 * bossMultiplier</code> XP instead.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>countDangerSpikes(int numRooms, int seed, int threshold)</code></h3>
    <p>
        For each room <code>r</code> from <code>1</code> through <code>numRooms</code> inclusive,
        the procedural generator computes a danger rating:
    </p>
    <pre><code>int danger = (r * seed + 17) % 100;</code></pre>
    <p>
        Return the number of rooms whose danger rating is greater than or equal to
        <code>threshold</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The Amnesiac Accumulator &amp; Inclusive Bounds</h3>
        <p>
            Compare these two broken implementations of <code>totalScaledXP</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Bug A: Where is total declared?
for (int f = startFloor; f &lt;= endFloor; f++) {
    int total = 0;
    total += f * 100;
}

// Bug B: Look at the loop test
int total = 0;
for (int f = startFloor; f &lt; endFloor; f++) {
    ...
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>In Bug A, why does declaring <code>int total = 0;</code> inside the loop body cause
                both a compiler scope error after the loop AND reset your running sum on every floor?</li>
            <li>In Bug B, if <code>startFloor = 1</code> and <code>endFloor = 5</code>, why does
                <code>f &lt; endFloor</code> miss the Boss Floor at floor 5 completely?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>longestSafeStreak(int numRooms, int trapCycle, int bossRoom)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Streak-finding is one of the College Board's favorite AP FRQ 1 patterns. You walk through
        rooms <code>1</code> to <code>numRooms</code> inclusive:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>A room <code>r</code> is <strong>unsafe</strong> if <code>r == bossRoom</code> OR
            <code>r % trapCycle == 0</code>.</li>
        <li>Otherwise, room <code>r</code> is <strong>safe</strong>.</li>
        <li>Return the length of the <strong>longest consecutive streak</strong> of safe rooms.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 2: The Trailing Streak Bug</h3>
        <p>
            Watch how many programmers write their streak tracker:
        </p>
        <pre><code>int currentStreak = 0;
int maxStreak = 0;
for (int r = 1; r &lt;= numRooms; r++) {
    if (r == bossRoom || r % trapCycle == 0) {
        if (currentStreak &gt; maxStreak) {
            maxStreak = currentStreak;
        }
        currentStreak = 0;
    } else {
        currentStreak++;
    }
}
return maxStreak;</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace that code on <code>longestSafeStreak(10, 6, 2)</code>: Room 2 is the boss and
                Room 6 is a trap, so Rooms <code>7, 8, 9, 10</code> are all safe (a streak of
                <code>4</code> at the very end of the dungeon!). What does the code above return,
                and why was <code>maxStreak</code> never updated for Rooms 7–10?</li>
            <li>How can you structure your update of <code>maxStreak</code> so that a streak ending
                on the final room is always captured?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>simulateGauntletHP(int startingHP, int numRooms, int trapCycle, int healCycle)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Simulate a hero starting with <code>startingHP</code> walking through rooms
        <code>1</code> through <code>numRooms</code> in order. In each room <code>r</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>If <code>r</code> is divisible by <strong>BOTH</strong> <code>trapCycle</code> and
            <code>healCycle</code>: it is a Cursed Fountain! Subtract <code>5</code> HP.</li>
        <li>Else if <code>r</code> is divisible by <code>trapCycle</code>: subtract
            <code>r * 2</code> HP.</li>
        <li>Else if <code>r</code> is divisible by <code>healCycle</code>: add <code>6</code> HP.</li>
        <li>Otherwise: subtract <code>1</code> HP for travel fatigue.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        <strong>Critical Death Rule:</strong> At the end of each room, if the hero's HP has dropped
        to <code>0</code> or below, the hero dies immediately — return <code>0</code> right away so
        a future healing room cannot resurrect a dead hero!
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Post-Mortem Healing &amp; FizzBuzz Ordering</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why would waiting until <em>after</em> the <code>for</code> loop finishes to check
                <code>if (hp &lt;= 0) return 0;</code> fail Check 2 in Part 4
                (<code>simulateGauntletHP(5, 4, 2, 3)</code>)? Trace the hero's HP in Room 1, Room
                2, and Room 3 to see what happens if the loop keeps running after death.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java EncounterSimCheck</code> reports <strong><code>16 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/for-loops.html">for Loops</a> — counting patterns, scope, accumulators</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while vs for</a> — choosing the right loop</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
